notes.body:
Can this share some code with the other http cases? E.g., I can imagine a helper that takes `tvb`, `pinfo`, `tree`, and `hfindex` as parameters so this could say something like

```c
static int
dissect_git_upload_pack_req(...)
{
  return dissect_http_pkt_lines(tvb, pinfo, tree, hf_git_upload_pack_req);
}
```

Does this get called more than once? If so, we can reduce indentation and avoid suspense by putting

```
if (inited)
  return;
inited = TRUE;
```

at the top of the function body instead of having this `if (!inited)` block. Alternatively, I wonder if we can skip that entirely and make the content of this block unconditional.

This seems a little different from how `git_handle` works: which approach do we prefer?
* `git_handle` is a file-global static, initialized in proto_register_git
* `git_upload_pack_adv_handle is a static scoped to proto_reg_handoff_git and is initialized there

I think `proto_register_git` is meant primarily to set up preferences, so the latter indeed seems like the right place. The function could use a bit of cleanup to explain what's going on --- e.g.

```
void
proto_reg_handoff_git(void)
{
  /* https://www.kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/technical/http-protocol.html */
  dissector_handle_t upload_pack_adv_handle = create_dissector_handle(...);
  dissector_handle_t upload_pack_req_handle = create_dissector_handle(...);
  dissector_handle_t upload_pack_res_handle = create_dissector_handle(...);

  dissector_add_string("media_type", ...);
  dissector_add_string("media_type", ...);
  dissector_add_string("media_type", ...);

  dissector_add_uint_with_preference("tcp.port", TCP_PORT_GIT, git_handle);
}

Close. This should still be in the `if`, or else we would end up parsing only one pkt-line in the success case.

That is,

```c
if (...) {
  proto_tree_add_expert(...);
  break;
}
```

